    "selected_installer_version": "1.1.2",
    "reason_installer": "firstStableVersion(from:what:) (line 505-517) returns the first list entry whose own `stable` field is true, falling back to list[0] only when none are stable; 1.1.2 is both the first entry and the first stable one here"
  },
  "notes": "Two structurally different \"first stable\" scans feeding the same download. The loader list (`versions/loader/{game}`) is an array of `{loader: {...}, intermediary: {...}, launcherMeta: {...}}` objects; the code reads `((loaderEntry[\"loader\"] as? [String:Any])?[\"stable\"] as? Bool) == true` on the FIRST matching entry (ServerJarProviders.swift:438), not the max build number -- the two happen to coincide in this real response (build 3 / 0.19.3 is both first and highest), which the fixture notes explicitly so a Rust port isn't written to sort-by-build-descending instead of scan-for-first-stable, a change that would be invisible against this one response but wrong in general. The installer list is a flatter `{version, stable}` array and uses the shared private helper `firstStableVersion(from:what:)`, reused identically for the game-version resolution in `downloadLatest` (not separately fixtured here -- same helper, already covered by fabric-first-stable-version-falls-back-to-first-entry-when-none-stable for its fallback branch).",
